course: dictionary
lesson: 4137
title: Big words, big world: globalisation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Globalisation is a noun, and it is built from the word global, which means worldwide. It means countries around the world are becoming more and more connected. «One phone can have parts from 10 countries that travel a total of about 100,000 miles.» The sentence tells you the parts come from many countries, so globalisation must be about the whole world being linked.
explain: Ask yourself: is the sentence about one place, or many? Here it is about many countries sharing parts, jobs and goods. Try your meaning in the sentence: «One phone can have parts from 10 countries that are connected through trade.» It still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
explain: The word can also mean big companies selling the same things in many countries. When you see a famous shop or drink in lots of places around the world, that is globalisation at work. Clues like countries, trade, travel and companies help you spot it.
fact: Globalisation means countries around the world are becoming more connected.
fact: It spreads through trade, travel, the internet, money and ideas.
fact: «Globalisation» can also mean big companies selling the same things in many countries.
fact: It can bring jobs and cheaper goods, but it can also close factories in one place and cause pollution from shipping.
fact: The US spelling is «globalization».
exercise: «Globalisation is countries being connected through trade.» What does globalisation mean? | Only one country trading with itself | A map of the world drawn by hand | *Countries around the world becoming more connected | Making things by hand
exercise: Which of these is a sign of globalisation? | *Your trainers were made in Vietnam | Your gran knits a jumper at home | A village shop sells only local bread | A family walks to the nearby park
exercise: Which is a possible downside of globalisation? | People travelling to see new places | Sharing of music and food ideas | Cheaper goods in the shops | *Factories closing in one place
test: Which is the US spelling of globalisation? | globalsation | globalisation | *globalization | globalation
test: What does globalisation mean? | The weather of the whole world | *Countries around the world becoming more connected through trade, travel and ideas | Shops closing on Sundays | A single country with its own money
exam: Dev says globalisation means one shop in one town. Why is he wrong? | Globalisation only happens in schools | Globalisation is only about the weather | Globalisation means a town gets bigger | *Globalisation is about countries around the world being connected through trade and travel
